nodecode-network-xray is a rewrite of rusty4444's rusty4444/hermes-network-xray:

  https://github.com/rusty4444/hermes-network-xray
  commit 4c28b7be6116e150b41ce2e62571c63bfc0968ae
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Sam Russell (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's evidence model and its four model-facing tools. A
probe is still one direct connection -- no proxy, no redirect followed, no
body, no credential -- walking DNS, TCP, verified TLS and one HTTP HEAD, with
each stage's answer kept on the record; the bundle's fields are the
original's (schema_version, kind, id, captured_at, vantage, target, policy,
capture, stages, integrity), so a bundle the original's portable collector
wrote imports here and a record taken here reads the same way. The tools keep
their names (network_xray_probe, network_xray_import, network_xray_compare,
network_xray_history), their arguments (url, label, connect_ip, bundle_json,
left_id, right_id) and their answers (ok and result, or ok false and one
line saying what to check), and the comparison keeps the original's verdicts
(both_healthy, both_failed, diverged, inconclusive), its stage-by-stage
findings, its hypotheses, its one manual follow-up with
requires_confirmation, and its limitations. The Python became Common Lisp:
sockets are usocket's, the verified handshake is cl+ssl's with the peer's
protocol and issuer read through OpenSSL, the JSON is the kernel's, the
content digest is ironclad's SHA-256 over canonical bytes, and the records
are one JSON file per probe in the cell's own directory. Left out: the
Desktop workbench page and its React bundle, the standalone HTML report
export, the CLI's own subcommands and the portable collector's stdin/stdout
protocol -- a Nodecode cell is called from a turn or from /network-xray, and
a peer's evidence arrives as the bundle JSON it wrote. The TLS stage records
the negotiated protocol and the issuer instead of a certificate fingerprint,
because the image's TLS library exposes no portable accessor for one; and the
comparison of a certificate therefore reads that protocol, which is what
first_divergence needed either way. Added, because a cell lives in one
profile rather than in a plugin folder: the `network-xray` section (a
required vantage label, the stage timeout, the history limit and the records
directory), a /network-xray command that prints the same ladder as lines,
and (help :network-xray).
